### What does backoff with jitter solve?

Without backoff, 1000 clients that fail at the same instant all retry at the same instant (thundering herd). Exponential backoff with jitter spaces the retries out so the dependency is not hammered with a synchronized burst.

```java
package com.bank.resilience;

import java.util.Random;

public class BackoffWithJitter {
    private static final Random rng = new Random();

    public static long calculateDelay(int attempt) {
        long baseMs = (long) Math.pow(2, attempt) * 100;
        long jitterMs = rng.nextInt(100);
        return baseMs + jitterMs;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) {
            System.out.println("Attempt " + i + ": delay ~" + calculateDelay(i) + " ms");
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output (approximate):
Attempt 0: delay ~150 ms
Attempt 1: delay ~270 ms
Attempt 2: delay ~450 ms
Attempt 3: delay ~870 ms
```

### Why does backoff not solve idempotency?

Retrying a non-idempotent operation is unsafe regardless of how elegantly the attempts are spaced. The first charge may have succeeded but timed out, so a retry charges the customer twice.

```java
package com.bank.resilience;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class IdempotencyProblem {

    // WRONG: retry a non-idempotent charge
    public void chargeUnsafe(String cardId, BigDecimal amount) {
        for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
            try {
                // paymentApi.charge(cardId, amount);
                System.out.println("Attempt " + i + ": charge " + amount);
                break;
            } catch (Exception e) {
                System.out.println("Attempt " + i + " timed out. Retrying...");
                // First charge may have succeeded but timed out
                // Retry charges TWICE!
            }
        }
    }

    // CORRECT: make the operation idempotent
    public void chargeSafe(String idempotencyKey, String cardId, BigDecimal amount) {
        for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
            try {
                // paymentApi.chargeIdempotent(idempotencyKey, cardId, amount);
                System.out.println("Attempt " + i + ": charge with key " + idempotencyKey);
                System.out.println("Server deduplicates by key. Safe to retry.");
                break;
            } catch (Exception e) {
                System.out.println("Attempt " + i + " timed out. Retrying safely...");
            }
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        IdempotencyProblem svc = new IdempotencyProblem();
        System.out.println("--- WRONG: non-idempotent charge ---");
        svc.chargeUnsafe("CARD001", new BigDecimal("5000.00"));
        System.out.println("\n--- CORRECT: idempotent charge ---");
        svc.chargeSafe("idem-key-001", "CARD001", new BigDecimal("5000.00"));
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
--- WRONG: non-idempotent charge ---
Attempt 0: charge 5000.00

--- CORRECT: idempotent charge ---
Attempt 0: charge with key idem-key-001
Server deduplicates by key. Safe to retry.
```

### Why do multi-layer retries multiply?

When every layer in the call chain retries independently, the attempts multiply. Gateway retries 3 times, each hitting a service that retries 3 times, each hitting a client that retries 3 times. One request becomes 27 calls to the dependency.

```java
package com.bank.resilience;

public class RetryMultiplication {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int gatewayRetries = 3;
        int serviceRetries = 3;
        int clientRetries  = 3;
        int totalAttempts  = gatewayRetries * serviceRetries * clientRetries;

        System.out.println("Gateway retries: " + gatewayRetries);
        System.out.println("Service retries: " + serviceRetries);
        System.out.println("Client retries:  " + clientRetries);
        System.out.println("Total attempts:  " + totalAttempts + " (not 3!)");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Gateway retries: 3
Service retries: 3
Client retries:  3
Total attempts:  27 (not 3!)
```

### Who should own the retry decision?

One layer should own retries; inner layers fail fast. The edge (gateway) is usually the right layer because it knows the user's intent and deadline.

```java
package com.bank.resilience;

public class SingleLayerOwnership {

    // CORRECT: only the edge retries, inner layers fail fast
    public String edgeCall(String request) {
        for (int attempt = 0; attempt < 3; attempt++) {
            try {
                return innerServiceCallNoRetry(request);
            } catch (Exception e) {
                System.out.println("Edge retry " + attempt);
            }
        }
        return "FAILED after 3 edge retries";
    }

    // Inner service does NOT retry
    private String innerServiceCallNoRetry(String request) {
        throw new RuntimeException("Dependency timeout");
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SingleLayerOwnership svc = new SingleLayerOwnership();
        System.out.println(svc.edgeCall("transfer:SAV2001"));
        System.out.println("Total attempts to dependency: 3 (not 27)");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Edge retry 0
Edge retry 1
Edge retry 2
FAILED after 3 edge retries
Total attempts to dependency: 3 (not 27)
```

### What is a retry budget and why does it matter?

Retries consume real capacity from the dependency. A retry budget caps retries as a fraction of total traffic (e.g. 10%), so when failures are widespread the budget is spent immediately and the system stops amplifying.

```java
package com.bank.resilience;

import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicLong;

public class RetryBudget {
    private final AtomicLong requests = new AtomicLong();
    private final AtomicLong retries  = new AtomicLong();
    private final double maxRatio;

    public RetryBudget(double maxRatio) {
        this.maxRatio = maxRatio;
    }

    public void recordRequest() {
        requests.incrementAndGet();
    }

    public boolean allowRetry() {
        if (retries.get() + 1 > requests.get() * maxRatio) {
            return false; // budget spent
        }
        retries.incrementAndGet();
        return true;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        RetryBudget budget = new RetryBudget(0.10); // 10% budget
        for (int i = 0; i < 1000; i++) budget.recordRequest();

        int allowed = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < 500; i++) {
            if (budget.allowRetry()) allowed++;
        }
        System.out.println("500 failures, retries allowed: " + allowed);
        System.out.println("Budget capped at 10% of 1000 = 100 retries");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
500 failures, retries allowed: 100
Budget capped at 10% of 1000 = 100 retries
```

### How do all the controls fit together?

| Control | Solves |
|---|---|
| Backoff plus jitter | WHEN to retry (timing, avoid thundering herd) |
| Idempotency | WHETHER retry is correct (safe to repeat) |
| Single-layer ownership | WHO retries (no multiplication across layers) |
| Retry budget | WHETHER the dependency can afford it (cap amplification) |
| Circuit breaker | Total cutoff when dependency is clearly down |

```java
package com.bank.resilience;

public class CompleteRetryStrategy {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("1. Backoff + jitter:      WHEN to retry");
        System.out.println("2. Idempotency key:       WHETHER retry is safe");
        System.out.println("3. Single-layer ownership: WHO retries (edge only)");
        System.out.println("4. Retry budget (10%):    can dependency afford it?");
        System.out.println("5. Circuit breaker:       total cutoff when down");
        System.out.println("All five together = safe retry strategy");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
1. Backoff + jitter:      WHEN to retry
2. Idempotency key:       WHETHER retry is safe
3. Single-layer ownership: WHO retries (edge only)
4. Retry budget (10%):    can dependency afford it?
5. Circuit breaker:       total cutoff when down
All five together = safe retry strategy
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is treating "we have exponential backoff with jitter" as the complete retry strategy.

```java
package com.bank.resilience;

public class CommonMistakes {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // WRONG: "we have backoff with jitter" as entire strategy
        System.out.println("WRONG: backoff alone");
        System.out.println("Still retries non-idempotent ops");
        System.out.println("Still multiplies across 3 layers = 27 attempts");
        System.out.println("Still pours load onto overloaded dependency");

        // CORRECT: all five controls together
        System.out.println("\nCORRECT: backoff + idempotency + single-layer");
        System.out.println("+ retry budget + circuit breaker");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
WRONG: backoff alone
Still retries non-idempotent ops
Still multiplies across 3 layers = 27 attempts
Still pours load onto overloaded dependency

CORRECT: backoff + idempotency + single-layer
+ retry budget + circuit breaker
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- "We have exponential backoff with jitter" is not an answer to "is your retry policy safe."
- Backoff solves the easiest quarter of the problem; the dangerous parts are the three questions it never touches.
- Retries are consumption from a finite capacity budget, not free resilience.
- The edge (which knows the user's intent and deadline) is usually the right layer to own retries.

```java
package com.bank.resilience;

public class SeniorInsights {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Backoff solves 25% (timing). Remaining 75%:");
        System.out.println("  - Idempotency: is the operation safe to repeat?");
        System.out.println("  - Ownership: which layer retries? (edge)");
        System.out.println("  - Budget: can the dependency afford more traffic?");
        System.out.println("  - Circuit breaker: when to stop entirely?");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Backoff solves 25% (timing). Remaining 75%:
  - Idempotency: is the operation safe to repeat?
  - Ownership: which layer retries? (edge)
  - Budget: can the dependency afford more traffic?
  - Circuit breaker: when to stop entirely?
```
